{
  "date": "2026-11-14",
  "format": "dialogue",
  "hosts": {
    "A": { "name": "Maya", "voice": "marin" },
    "B": { "name": "Alex", "voice": "cedar" }
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "intro",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "It's Saturday, November 14th, and this is The AI Edge, presented by Epilogue." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Epilogue builds AI for work where being wrong is expensive. Epilogue ships systems that know what they know, show their work, and fail visibly instead of quietly. Visit epiloguelabs.com to learn more." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "I'm Maya." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And I'm Alex." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Here's what happened at the frontier of AI since yesterday morning. The advances, the research, and the uses for good and for harm, each one traced back to the source that reported it." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "First, the Federal Aviation Administration halted every departing flight in the United States on Friday afternoon, and more than 7,000 flights were cancelled before the ground stop was lifted that evening." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Second, the FBI issued a warning about an open-source agentic hacking framework called ARTEX, which it says has taken some intrusions from initial targeting to privileged access in under three hours." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And third, VulnCheck told The Register that this year has produced 100,222 published vulnerabilities, more than double last year's total, and blames AI vulnerability hunting for most of that." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Frontier models & labs",
      "headline": "OpenAI disbands its standalone robotics team, folding researchers into its main research and Applied organizations",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "We start at OpenAI. Fortune reports that it has disbanded its stand-alone robotics team." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Where did the people go?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "The robotics research team moves into the main research team under the chief research officer, Mark Chen. The applied robotics and hardware teams move into the Applied organization under the president, Greg Brockman." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And OpenAI's line on it?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "A spokesperson told Fortune that robotics remains a core part of OpenAI's long-term vision, and called the reorganisation a sign of commitment rather than a retreat. Fortune also cites an Nvidia study where an Astra-powered agent reached a 74.8% success rate on a simulated manipulation benchmark, against 51.0% for the best specialised robotic models on that leaderboard." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Those are the company's own words, and the benchmark figures are not independently verified. Fortune is a single source here, it doesn't name the benchmark, and it doesn't give a headcount for the team." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Frontier models & labs",
      "headline": "DeepSeek opens its full app feature set through the API on V4.1-Flash, with server-side tool calls",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "DeepSeek, meanwhile, published a release note on Friday opening its full app experience through the API." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "What does that actually hand developers?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "The model is V4.1-Flash, and it works across the Messages, Responses and Chat Completions interfaces, with the tool calls and the context assembly handled on DeepSeek's side. You get web search with citations, a deep thinking search that reads pages through multi-step reasoning, file input for PDFs and Office files and images, automatic URL reading, and safety checks that screen inputs and outputs." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Anything mandatory in there?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "DeepSeek says public-facing apps must enable safety checks, and that developers stay responsible for content moderation, model registration and labelling AI-generated content. There are no benchmark figures with the release, and how well those safety checks actually work is the company's own description, which has not been independently verified." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Research & papers",
      "headline": "Anthropic says API and claude.ai configurations differ by up to 34 percentage points on multi-turn safety evals",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "To the research, and Anthropic published a post on building safety evaluations where the headline number is about configuration, not capability." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Meaning what?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "On multi-turn safety evaluations, the appropriate response rate for Claude Fable 5.1 differed between the API and the claude.ai product by as much as 34 percentage points. On self-harm mitigation it was 60% on the API against 94% on claude.ai with the new system prompt." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Same model, different wrapper, and a very different safety number." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "That's the point of the post. Anthropic also reports harmless response rates between 94% and 98% for every recent model on single-turn API evaluations, and calls those saturated, useful now mainly as regression checks." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "What about the error bars?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Anthropic says confidence intervals on multi-turn API evaluations can exceed plus or minus 10 percentage points. On a romance-scams evaluation it reports 98% for Claude Opus 5 and 94% for Opus 5.5, and says that gap was not statistically significant, so it can't say which model did better. These are Anthropic's measurements of its own models, and they are not independently verified." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Research & papers",
      "headline": "Google Research says a multi-agent system proved a 2013 information-theory conjecture, formalised in 50 million lines of Lean 4",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "Google Research posted something unusual on Friday." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Which is?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "It says its Antigravity teamwork feature was used to prove information theory's 2013 Courtade-Kumar conjecture in full generality, and to formalise that proof in Lean 4, producing 50 million lines of code. It also reports over 919,000 lines of Lean synthesised for a formalisation related to Hilbert's tenth problem." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "That's a great deal of machine-written mathematics. What about the peer-review side?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Authors of 578 submissions to a theory conference opted into Google's Deep Review tool. In an ongoing survey of 70 respondents, 90% rated the feedback mostly or almost all factual, 90% said they would use it again, over 30% said it caught substantive proof bugs or significant typos, and 14% said it found technical gaps that took more than an hour to fix." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "How much of that can we check?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Very little. Every figure is Google's own, published on a company research blog rather than in a paper, and not independently verified. Google doesn't say the Lean formalisation has been checked by anyone outside the project, and for the chemistry result it doesn't give the single-agent baseline it's being compared against." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "That's the research. Now to security, which took up most of today's edition." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Security, misuse & threat intelligence",
      "headline": "FBI FLASH: ARTEX agents took some intrusions from initial targeting to privileged access in under three hours",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "The FBI issued a FLASH advisory on Friday about ARTEX, and this is an update to a story we've been following all week." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "What is ARTEX, in the FBI's own description?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "An open-source, large language model driven agentic platform that it says threat actors have weaponised to automate multiple phases of cyber intrusions at machine speed, from reconnaissance through data exfiltration. In use since at least August." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And how fast is machine speed, concretely?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "The FBI says some intrusions progressed from initial targeting to privileged access in under three hours. It names attacks on South Korean financial institutions that exfiltrated personal information from approximately 67,000 individuals, and a supply-chain compromise of ConnectWise Cloud Backup customers that enabled access to more than 2,250 downstream tenants." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Which models is it driving?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "It's written in Go and carries no model of its own. It integrates Anthropic Claude, OpenAI GPT, DeepSeek, GLM, Grok and Kimi. The FBI writes that Chinese models are most prevalent in attacker-controlled instances, with DeepSeek Flash family models dominating, citing their capabilities, their relatively low cost, and their lack of observable guardrails when the task is framed as penetration testing." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "The repository came down, though." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "On October 8th. But the FBI says the framework keeps spreading through forks, mirrors, archived copies and a commercial version, and that as of early November researchers identified over 1,600 publicly exposed ARTEX consoles. One detail worth holding onto: the FBI says ARTEX generates high volumes of forensically noisy traffic. Speed is the advantage here, not stealth." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Security, misuse & threat intelligence",
      "headline": "Amazon decoys posing as model APIs captured ARTEX agents' own operating memory from two campaigns",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "Amazon Web Services got a look inside those agents, and published it on its security blog." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "How did they manage that?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Amazon says its MadPot decoy sensors, dressed up as model inference APIs, captured requests from AI agents between October 3rd and November 5th that carried the agents' own operating memory. Task context, system prompts, tool histories, knowledge-graph state and reported findings." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "What were they working on?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Two campaigns. In the first, agents harvested AI model API keys and fed the stolen keys back into the framework's own model gateway, so the theft paid for more theft. They graded finds from a lead up to quantified impact, and treated keys exposed publicly for more than 30 days as low-value inventory." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And the second?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Identity verification at two unnamed cryptocurrency exchanges, using real-time deepfake video to pass live selfie checks. The pipeline reached 11.5 frames per second on a consumer-grade GPU, and the best attempt completed the movement sequence with the face positioned within 7 pixels of the target." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Did it get through?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "No. Amazon says the vendor's server-side verdict rejected the deepfake in every recorded attempt. And the caveats are Amazon's own: it rates the ARTEX link at only moderate confidence, it did not observe the activity directly, it could not confirm the outcomes the agents reported, and these figures are not independently verified." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Security, misuse & threat intelligence",
      "headline": "VulnCheck counts 100,222 CVEs in 2026, more than double 2025, and attributes the majority to AI vulnerability hunting",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "Then there's the volume number. The Register reports that VulnCheck counted 100,222 published vulnerabilities for this year, as of Friday afternoon, more than double last year's total." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And the cause?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "VulnCheck's director of coordinated vulnerability disclosure, Josh Bressers, told The Register there's no denying that vulnerability hunting with AI is driving a majority of what they're seeing." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Does that volume turn into exploitation?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "About 1.4%, or 1,363 of this year's vulnerabilities, are in VulnCheck's known-exploited catalogue. VulnCheck puts the median time to exploitation at 80 days for the first half of this year, down from 120 days last year." },
